  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM EDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\n- Mainly dry with moderating temperatures through Monday\n\nNothing much has changed regarding the upcoming extended dry and \nmild period expected for the area through early next week.\n\nWe do have one more weak wave rotating around the upper low to our \nsouth today that we have to contend with. The biggest impact with \nthis will be a shroud of lower clouds that rotates through the \nsouthern portion of the area. Maybe a sprinkle comes out of it, but \nthese clouds will not have much depth to them with a fairly strong \nsubsidence inversion building in ahead of the incoming ridge. The \ndry low level flow from the NE will not help in allowing rain to \nreach the sfc.\n\nWe see the ridge then move in on Thursday as the upper low moves far \nenough away. This transition, especially the upper low moving \noffshore and amplifying, will help to slow the progression of \nfeatures across the entire continent. We will see the ridge control \nour area from Thursday, right on through early Tuesday. A trough \ndoes try to approach Sunday. It will be sheared out as it encounters \nthe strong ridge and dry weather.\n\nTemperatures will slowly moderate through the period from the 60s \ntoday, to 70s over the weekend and early next week. This is \nsupported as 850 mb temperatures increase from around +6C today, to \n+12 to 13C over the weekend and early next week.\n\n- Next chance of rain comes in late next Tuesday\n\nWe will eventually see a stronger jet streak and long wave upper \ntrough push east across the country early next week. Indications and \nmean timing has rain chances coming in later next Tuesday at this \ntime. Would not be surprised to see this delayed a bit given the \nstrong ridging and very dry air in place that will resist the trough \na bit.",
